function prng(seed){var a=(seed>>>0)||1;return function(){a|=0;a=(a+0x6D2B79F5)|0;var t=Math.imul(a^(a>>>15),1|a);t=(t+Math.imul(t^(t>>>7),61|t))^t;return((t^(t>>>14))>>>0)/4294967296;};}
function pick(r,s){return r[(s()*r.length)|0];}
var PREFIX="JAH-QUE-";
var NAME="Queue";
var FIELDS=["overview", "operations", "implementation", "circular-buffer", "complexity", "interview", "variants", "real-world", "practice"];
var ARCHIVE=[{"id": "JAH-QUE-000001", "title": "Queue Overview: What It Is and Why It Matters", "d": "The queue: first in, first out — the fairest line ever invented.", "description": "A queue is an ordered collection with one strict rule: the first item added is the first item removed — first-in, first-out, or FIFO. Think of a checkout line at a store: new shoppers join at the back, the cashier serves the front, and nobody jumps ahead. The two operations are enqueue (join the back) and dequeue (leave from the front), plus peek to look at who's next without removing them.\n\nQueues matter because fairness and order are load-bearing ideas in computing. Operating systems schedule processes in queues; printers spool jobs in queues; network routers buffer packets in queues; breadth-first search explores graphs with a queue, which is exactly why it finds shortest paths. Anywhere work arrives over time and must be handled in arrival order, a queue is the honest structure.\n\nThe discipline is the whole point. A queue deliberately hides the middle — you cannot reach in and grab item five. That restriction is what makes queues analyzable: producers and consumers can work at different speeds with the queue absorbing the difference, and every consumer sees the same fair order. Learn the queue and you understand buffering, scheduling, and level-by-level traversal in one stroke.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000002", "title": "Queue: How It Works — Core Operations", "d": "Enqueue, dequeue, peek, and the two-pointer machinery that makes them O(1).", "description": "A queue needs to add at one end and remove from the other, both in O(1). The classic implementation uses an array with two indices: head (where the next dequeue reads) and tail (where the next enqueue writes). Enqueue writes at tail and advances it; dequeue reads at head and advances it. Neither operation touches any other element, so both are O(1) time and O(1) space.\n\nThe subtlety is that head marches forward and would eventually run off the end of the array. The circular buffer fixes this: indices wrap around with modulo arithmetic (head = (head + 1) % capacity), so the queue reuses the slots freed by dequeues and the storage stays bounded. When the buffer fills completely, the queue either blocks, drops, or grows — the policy choice defines bounded vs unbounded queues.\n\nPeek returns the head element without moving the pointer; isEmpty checks head == tail (with a size counter or a wasted slot to distinguish full from empty). Size is maintained as a counter, O(1), never recomputed. These mechanics are small but exact — get the wraparound or the full/empty distinction wrong and the queue corrupts silently.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000003", "title": "Queue: Internals, Trade-offs and When to Use It", "d": "Bounded vs unbounded, blocking vs dropping, and where queues beat every alternative.", "description": "Internally a queue is one of the simplest structures: an array plus two indices, or a linked list with head and tail pointers. The array version is cache-friendly and allocation-free after setup but needs a capacity policy; the linked-list version grows without bound but pays a pointer and an allocation per element. Both give O(1) enqueue and dequeue — the choice is about memory behavior, not complexity.\n\nThe real design decisions are policies, not data structures. Bounded queues pick a capacity and then choose: block the producer when full (backpressure — the honest choice for pipelines), drop the newest (tail drop), drop the oldest (head drop, for fresh-data workloads like sensor streams), or grow (unbounded — simple until memory runs out). Thread-safe queues add locking or lock-free atomics around the two pointers.\n\nReach for a queue when order of arrival must be preserved, producers and consumers run at different rates, or you need level-by-level processing (BFS, print spooling, task scheduling). Reach past it when you need priorities (priority queue), both ends (deque), or key lookup (hash table). The queue's restriction — no middle access — is a feature: it makes concurrent reasoning and fairness guarantees possible.", "ladder": 3, "group": "overview", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000004", "title": "Queue Enqueue", "d": "Joining the back of the line: O(1), always.", "description": "Enqueue adds an element at the tail. In the circular-buffer implementation, that means writing to slots[tail] and advancing tail = (tail + 1) % capacity — two operations, no element moves, no searching. It is O(1) time and O(1) extra space, unconditionally: unlike array append there is no amortization subtlety, because the buffer is pre-allocated and the write position is always known.\n\nThe only question is what happens when the queue is full. An unbounded queue grows the buffer (O(n) copy, amortized O(1) per enqueue — the same doubling protocol as dynamic arrays). A bounded queue applies its policy: block, drop newest, or drop oldest. Python's collections.deque with maxlen drops the oldest automatically; queue.Queue blocks by default. Know your queue's full policy before you trust it in a pipeline.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "enqueue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "write at tail, advance with wraparound; no element moves"}, {"op": "enqueue (growing)", "complexity": "O(1) amortized", "note": "unbounded queues double the buffer when full"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nq = deque()\nq.append('job-1')   # enqueue at the back — O(1)\nq.append('job-2')\nq.append('job-3')\nprint(list(q))      # ['job-1', 'job-2', 'job-3']", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1): the tail position is always known; nothing else moves. Full-buffer policy (block/drop/grow) is the only design decision.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000005", "title": "Queue Dequeue", "d": "Serving the front of the line: O(1), the FIFO guarantee made concrete.", "description": "Dequeue removes and returns the head element — the one that has waited longest. In the circular buffer, that means reading slots[head] and advancing head = (head + 1) % capacity. Like enqueue it touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space. The FIFO guarantee is just the observation that head only moves forward through arrival order, so the longest-waiting element is always served next.\n\nDequeueing from an empty queue is the error case: safe implementations raise (or block, in blocking queues) rather than returning garbage. Always guard with isEmpty in manual implementations, or use the blocking variants when consumers should wait for work. The freed slot is immediately reusable by a future enqueue — that reuse is the entire point of the circular layout.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "dequeue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read at head, advance with wraparound; the freed slot is reused"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nq = deque(['job-1', 'job-2', 'job-3'])\nprint(q.popleft())  # job-1 — longest waiting, served first\nprint(q.popleft())  # job-2\nprint(list(q))      # ['job-3']", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1): one read, one pointer advance. Empty-dequeue must raise or block — never return garbage.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000006", "title": "Queue Peek and Front", "d": "Seeing who's next without serving them: O(1) and non-destructive.", "description": "Peek (front) returns the head element without advancing the pointer — a read-only look at who's next. It costs O(1) and changes nothing, which makes it the safe way to inspect before deciding: a scheduler can peek at the next job's priority, a parser can peek at the next token, a BFS can check the frontier's depth. Like dequeue, peeking an empty queue is an error.\n\nBack (rear) peek is the deque's extra: looking at the most recently enqueued element. A pure queue doesn't need it, but many implementations expose it because the tail pointer is right there. The discipline matters more than the API: peek lets you look, only dequeue lets you consume, and that separation is what keeps producer–consumer protocols honest.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "peek / front", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read slots[head] without moving the pointer"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nq = deque(['a', 'b', 'c'])\nprint(q[0])     # a — peek at the front, nothing removed\nprint(len(q))   # 3 — size unchanged\nprint(q[-1])    # c — peek at the back (deque extra)", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1), non-destructive. Peek-then-decide is the core pattern of schedulers and parsers.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000007", "title": "Queue isEmpty and Size", "d": "O(1) bookkeeping: a counter beats recounting every time.", "description": "isEmpty and size look trivial, but their implementation decides a real bug class. In a circular buffer, head == tail is ambiguous — it means both empty and full. The standard fixes are a size counter (increment on enqueue, decrement on dequeue) or a permanently wasted slot (declare full when the next tail would hit head). The counter is clearer and makes size O(1); recomputing size by walking would be O(n) and is never acceptable.\n\nGuard every dequeue and peek with isEmpty. In concurrent queues, the check-and-act must be atomic — 'if not empty: dequeue' is a race unless the queue itself synchronizes it, which is why blocking queues expose get() variants that wait instead of racing. Sequential code: keep a counter. Concurrent code: use the queue's own synchronization, never your own check.", "ladder": 1, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "isEmpty", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "size counter == 0; or head == tail with empty/full disambiguation"}, {"op": "size", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "maintained counter — never recomputed by walking"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nq = deque()\nprint(len(q) == 0)  # True — isEmpty\nq.append('x')\nprint(len(q))       # 1 — O(1) counter, not a walk", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1) via a maintained counter. head == tail alone is ambiguous (empty vs full) — the counter or a wasted slot resolves it.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000008", "title": "Circular Buffer Mechanics", "d": "The wraparound array: modulo arithmetic that reuses freed slots forever.", "description": "The circular buffer is the queue's ideal body: a fixed array where indices wrap around with modulo arithmetic. Enqueue writes at tail and sets tail = (tail + 1) % capacity; dequeue reads at head and sets head = (head + 1) % capacity. The elements always occupy a contiguous logical run from head to tail, which may physically wrap around the end of the array — slots[head..cap) followed by slots[0..tail). No element ever moves; only the two pointers orbit.\n\nWork through the wraparound once by hand and it clicks forever: capacity 5, enqueue a,b,c (head=0, tail=3), dequeue twice (head=2), enqueue d,e,f — tail wraps from 4 to 0 to 1, and the queue holds [c,d,e,f] in slots 2,3,4,0. The freed slots 0 and 1 were reused without any copying. Distinguishing full from empty needs the size counter or one wasted slot; getting this wrong is the classic circular-buffer bug, so test the exact full→enqueue and empty→dequeue transitions.", "ladder": 2, "group": "circular-buffer", "operations": [{"op": "enqueue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "slots[tail] = x; tail = (tail + 1) % capacity"}, {"op": "dequeue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "x = slots[head]; head = (head + 1) % capacity"}], "program": "class CircularQueue:\n    def __init__(self, cap):\n        self.a = [None] * cap\n        self.head = self.tail = self.n = 0\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        if self.n == len(self.a): raise OverflowError('full')\n        self.a[self.tail] = x\n        self.tail = (self.tail + 1) % len(self.a)\n        self.n += 1\n    def dequeue(self):\n        if self.n == 0: raise IndexError('empty')\n        x = self.a[self.head]\n        self.head = (self.head + 1) % len(self.a)\n        self.n -= 1\n        return x\n\nq = CircularQueue(3)\nfor v in 'abc': q.enqueue(v)\nprint(q.dequeue(), q.dequeue())  # a b — slots 0,1 freed\nq.enqueue('d')  # reuses slot 0 via wraparound\nprint(q.dequeue(), q.dequeue())  # c d", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1) both ends, zero element moves, fixed memory. The size counter disambiguates full from empty.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000009", "title": "Bounded vs Unbounded Queues", "d": "Capacity policy is the design: block, drop, or grow when full.", "description": "A bounded queue picks a capacity up front. When it's full, someone must decide: block the producer until space frees (backpressure — the pipeline slows down honestly instead of exploding), drop the newest arrival (tail drop — fine when stale data is worthless), or drop the oldest (head drop — keeps the freshest data, right for sensor streams and live dashboards). Python's queue.Queue(maxsize=N) blocks; deque(maxlen=N) drops the oldest silently.\n\nAn unbounded queue grows instead — usually by doubling like a dynamic array, amortized O(1) per enqueue. It's simpler to reason about until a fast producer and a slow consumer turn it into a memory leak with extra steps: the queue grows forever and the process dies. Rule of thumb: bounded queues with explicit policy for production pipelines (failures stay local and visible), unbounded queues for algorithms like BFS where the bound is the input size itself.", "ladder": 2, "group": "circular-buffer", "operations": [{"op": "enqueue (bounded, full)", "complexity": "O(1) decision", "note": "block / drop-newest / drop-oldest / grow — pick deliberately"}, {"op": "enqueue (unbounded, full)", "complexity": "O(1) amortized", "note": "double the buffer like a dynamic array"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nfresh = deque(maxlen=3)   # bounded: drops OLDEST when full\nfor v in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]:\n    fresh.append(v)\nprint(list(fresh))  # [3, 4, 5] — 1 and 2 were evicted\n\nunbounded = deque()       # grows forever — fine for BFS, risky for pipelines\nunbounded.append('work')\nprint(list(unbounded))", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Policy, not complexity: all full-policies are O(1) to apply. Unbounded growth is amortized O(1) per enqueue via doubling.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000010", "title": "Queue via Two Stacks", "d": "Two LIFO stacks simulate a FIFO queue: amortized O(1), a beloved interview classic.", "description": "Keep an inbox stack and an outbox stack. Enqueue pushes onto inbox. Dequeue pops from outbox — but if outbox is empty, first pour the entire inbox into outbox (popping inbox, pushing outbox), which reverses the order, and then pop. The pour moves each element exactly once per cycle: an element is pushed to inbox once, moved to outbox once, and popped once — 3 touches total across its lifetime, so n operations cost O(n): amortized O(1) each.\n\nThe worst single dequeue is O(n) when it triggers the pour, which is why this is amortized rather than worst-case. It's still a beautiful result: two restricted structures simulating a third, proving that stacks and queues are computationally equivalent in power. Use it in interviews and in languages that hand you a stack but not a queue — in production, use the real queue.", "ladder": 3, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "enqueue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "push onto inbox stack"}, {"op": "dequeue", "complexity": "O(1) amortized", "note": "pop outbox; pour inbox->outbox when outbox is empty (O(n) rare)"}], "program": "class QueueViaStacks:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n    def dequeue(self):\n        if not self.outbox:\n            while self.inbox:\n                self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n        if not self.outbox: raise IndexError('empty')\n        return self.outbox.pop()\n\nq = QueueViaStacks()\nfor v in [1, 2, 3]: q.enqueue(v)\nprint(q.dequeue(), q.dequeue(), q.dequeue())  # 1 2 3 — FIFO from two LIFOs", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "Amortized O(1): each element moves inbox->outbox at most once. Worst single dequeue O(n).", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000011", "title": "Deque Operations", "d": "The double-ended queue: O(1) at both ends, the queue's bigger sibling.", "description": "A deque (double-ended queue) allows enqueue and dequeue at both ends: append/appendleft, pop/popleft, all O(1). It's implemented as a doubly-linked list or — as in CPython — a doubly-linked list of fixed-size blocks, which gives O(1) ends with good cache behavior and no wraparound bookkeeping. Every queue is a deque with one end tied behind its back; every stack is a deque using one end.\n\nReach for the deque when the algorithm needs both ends: palindromes, sliding-window maximum, undo buffers (push states, pop to undo, drop oldest when capped), and work-stealing schedulers. Python's collections.deque is the default serious queue — q.append/q.popleft are both O(1), unlike list.pop(0) which is O(n). When someone says 'queue' in Python, they usually mean deque.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "append / appendleft", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "enqueue at either end"}, {"op": "pop / popleft", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "dequeue from either end"}, {"op": "peek either end", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "q[0] / q[-1]"}], "program": "from collections import deque\nd = deque()\nd.append('back')        # enqueue right\nd.appendleft('front')   # enqueue left\nprint(list(d))          # ['front', 'back']\nprint(d.pop())          # back — dequeue right\nprint(d.popleft())      # front — dequeue left", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "O(1) at both ends. CPython uses linked blocks: no wraparound math, no resize pauses.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000012", "title": "Queue Implementation in Python", "d": "A production-style queue: circular buffer, full policy, and a deque-backed variant.", "description": "This record builds the circular-buffer queue from the operations section into a complete class with a bounded capacity, explicit full/empty errors, and O(1) size — then shows the idiomatic alternative, collections.deque, which you should use in real code. The hand-built version exists to make the head/tail wraparound concrete; the deque version exists because it's implemented in C, battle-tested, and thread-safe for appends and pops.\n\nThe demo runs the wraparound scenario: fill, drain halfway, refill past the physical end, and drain — proving slots get reused. Then it demonstrates the deque's maxlen drop-oldest policy. Read the manual version, ship the deque version.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class BoundedQueue:\n    \"\"\"Circular-buffer queue with explicit full/empty errors.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self._a = [None] * capacity\n        self._head = self._tail = self._n = 0\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        if self._n == len(self._a):\n            raise OverflowError(\"queue full\")\n        self._a[self._tail] = x\n        self._tail = (self._tail + 1) % len(self._a)\n        self._n += 1\n    def dequeue(self):\n        if self._n == 0:\n            raise IndexError(\"queue empty\")\n        x = self._a[self._head]\n        self._head = (self._head + 1) % len(self._a)\n        self._n -= 1\n        return x\n    def peek(self):\n        if self._n == 0:\n            raise IndexError(\"queue empty\")\n        return self._a[self._head]\n    def __len__(self):\n        return self._n\n\ndef main():\n    q = BoundedQueue(3)\n    for v in [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]:\n        q.enqueue(v)\n    print(\"peek:\", q.peek(), \"size:\", len(q))\n    print(\"serve:\", q.dequeue(), q.dequeue())\n    q.enqueue(\"d\")  # wraps into the freed slot\n    q.enqueue(\"e\")\n    while len(q):\n        print(\"serve:\", q.dequeue())\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "enqueue/dequeue/peek O(1); fixed memory = capacity slots. Manual build for learning; collections.deque for shipping.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000013", "title": "Queue Implementation in JavaScript", "d": "A queue in JavaScript: head-index array plus the idiomatic array shift.", "description": "JavaScript arrays make a simple queue trivial — push to enqueue, shift to dequeue — but shift is O(n) because it re-indexes every element. This implementation uses the head-index trick instead: keep an array and a head offset, enqueue with push, dequeue by reading a[head++] — amortized O(1), with periodic compaction when the head offset grows large. It mirrors the circular buffer's insight (never move elements) without the modulo bookkeeping.\n\nRun with node. The demo enqueues, dequeues, and shows the head offset advancing. For real code, this pattern is fine at moderate scale; at high throughput, a proper ring buffer or a library deque avoids the compaction pauses. The lesson ports everywhere: separate the logical queue from the physical storage.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "class Queue {\n  constructor() { this._a = []; this._head = 0; }\n  enqueue(x) { this._a.push(x); }\n  dequeue() {\n    if (this._head >= this._a.length) throw new Error(\"queue empty\");\n    const x = this._a[this._head++];\n    if (this._head > 1024 && this._head * 2 > this._a.length) {\n      this._a = this._a.slice(this._head); this._head = 0; // compact\n    }\n    return x;\n  }\n  peek() {\n    if (this._head >= this._a.length) throw new Error(\"queue empty\");\n    return this._a[this._head];\n  }\n  get size() { return this._a.length - this._head; }\n}\nfunction main() {\n  const q = new Queue();\n  [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"].forEach(v => q.enqueue(v));\n  console.log(\"peek:\", q.peek(), \"size:\", q.size);\n  console.log(\"serve:\", q.dequeue(), q.dequeue());\n  q.enqueue(\"d\");\n  while (q.size) console.log(\"serve:\", q.dequeue());\n}\nmain();\n", "language": "javascript", "complexity_notes": "enqueue/dequeue amortized O(1) via head offset; periodic compaction keeps memory bounded. Avoid Array.shift in loops (O(n)).", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000014", "title": "Queue Implementation in Java", "d": "A generic circular queue in Java, plus the standard ArrayDeque.", "description": "Java ships excellent queues — ArrayDeque for sequential code, ArrayBlockingQueue and LinkedBlockingQueue for concurrent code — but the learning build is a generic circular buffer, the same design as the Python version. Java's generics need the Object[] backing array with a cast on read, the standard workaround, and the full/empty states throw IllegalStateException / NoSuchElementException following the java.util.Queue conventions.\n\nCompile with javac CQueue.java and run with java CQueue. The demo mirrors the other languages. In production, reach for ArrayDeque (resizable circular array, O(1) ends, no capacity limits) for single-threaded work and ArrayBlockingQueue when threads share the queue — hand-rolled queues are for learning, not shipping.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "import java.util.NoSuchElementException;\npublic class CQueue\u003cT> {\n    private final Object[] a;\n    private int head, tail, n;\n    public CQueue(int capacity) { a = new Object[capacity]; }\n    public void enqueue(T x) {\n        if (n == a.length) throw new IllegalStateException(\"queue full\");\n        a[tail] = x; tail = (tail + 1) % a.length; n++;\n    }\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n    public T dequeue() {\n        if (n == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException(\"queue empty\");\n        T x = (T) a[head]; head = (head + 1) % a.length; n--;\n        return x;\n    }\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")\n    public T peek() {\n        if (n == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException(\"queue empty\");\n        return (T) a[head];\n    }\n    public int size() { return n; }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CQueue\u003cString> q = new CQueue\u003c>(3);\n        q.enqueue(\"a\"); q.enqueue(\"b\"); q.enqueue(\"c\");\n        System.out.println(\"peek=\" + q.peek() + \" size=\" + q.size());\n        System.out.println(\"serve=\" + q.dequeue() + \",\" + q.dequeue());\n        q.enqueue(\"d\"); q.enqueue(\"e\");\n        while (q.size() > 0) System.out.println(\"serve=\" + q.dequeue());\n    }\n}\n", "language": "java", "complexity_notes": "enqueue/dequeue O(1) circular. Production: ArrayDeque (single-threaded), ArrayBlockingQueue (concurrent).", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000015", "title": "Queue Implementation in C++", "d": "A template circular queue in C++: manual storage, exact capacity, zero waste.", "description": "C++ gives the queue its most economical body: a raw array of exactly capacity slots, two indices, one counter — no per-element overhead, no allocator traffic after construction. This template implementation follows the circular protocol with exceptions for full/empty, and the demo runs the standard wraparound scenario. std::queue (a deque adapter) and boost::circular_buffer are the production answers; this build is the understanding.\n\nCompile with g++ -std=c++17 -O2 cqueue.cpp -o cqueue and run ./cqueue. Note the modulo arithmetic on every operation — on most hardware it's a single instruction and disappears into the noise. The fixed capacity is a feature in embedded and real-time contexts: the queue's memory footprint is known at construction and can never grow, which is exactly what you want when allocation is forbidden.", "ladder": 2, "group": "implementation", "program": "#include \u003ciostream>\n#include \u003cstdexcept>\ntemplate \u003ctypename T>\nclass CQueue {\n    T* a; size_t cap, head, tail, n;\npublic:\n    explicit CQueue(size_t capacity)\n        : a(new T[capacity]), cap(capacity), head(0), tail(0), n(0) {}\n    ~CQueue() { delete[] a; }\n    void enqueue(const T& x) {\n        if (n == cap) throw std::overflow_error(\"queue full\");\n        a[tail] = x; tail = (tail + 1) % cap; ++n;\n    }\n    T dequeue() {\n        if (n == 0) throw std::underflow_error(\"queue empty\");\n        T x = a[head]; head = (head + 1) % cap; --n;\n        return x;\n    }\n    const T& peek() const {\n        if (n == 0) throw std::underflow_error(\"queue empty\");\n        return a[head];\n    }\n    size_t size() const { return n; }\n};\nint main() {\n    CQueue\u003cstd::string> q(3);\n    q.enqueue(\"a\"); q.enqueue(\"b\"); q.enqueue(\"c\");\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"peek=\" \u003c\u003c q.peek() \u003c\u003c \" size=\" \u003c\u003c q.size() \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n    std::cout \u003c\u003c \"serve=\" \u003c\u003c q.dequeue() \u003c\u003c \",\" \u003c\u003c q.dequeue() \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n    q.enqueue(\"d\"); q.enqueue(\"e\");\n    while (q.size()) std::cout \u003c\u003c \"serve=\" \u003c\u003c q.dequeue() \u003c\u003c \"\\n\";\n}\n", "language": "cpp", "complexity_notes": "O(1) ends, exact-capacity storage, no allocation after construction. std::queue / boost::circular_buffer for production.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000016", "title": "Queue Complexity Deep-Dive: Why Both Ends Are O(1)", "d": "The pointer argument: no operation ever visits more than one element.", "description": "The O(1) bounds for queues are unusually clean because they rest on a pointer argument, not amortization. Enqueue writes exactly one slot and advances one index. Dequeue reads exactly one slot and advances one index. Peek reads one slot. Size reads one counter. No operation's work grows with n, so every bound is worst-case O(1), not amortized — the only exception is the unbounded queue's doubling resize, which is amortized O(1) by the same accounting argument as dynamic arrays.\n\nCompare with the structures around it: array insert/delete are O(n) because elements must shift; linked-list insertion is O(1) only with a node reference in hand. The queue's restriction — ends only — is precisely what buys the unconditional O(1): the structure never needs to find, shift, or reorder anything. This is a general design lesson — the most constrained interface earns the strongest guarantees — and the queue is its purest example.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "operations": [{"op": "enqueue / dequeue / peek / size", "complexity": "O(1) worst-case", "note": "single slot + single index advance; no dependence on n"}, {"op": "enqueue (unbounded resize)", "complexity": "O(1) amortized", "note": "doubling; same accounting as dynamic arrays"}], "complexity_notes": "Worst-case O(1) for all core ops on fixed circular buffers; the constraint (ends-only access) is what makes the guarantee unconditional.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000017", "title": "Interview: Queue Using Two Stacks", "d": "Implement FIFO with two LIFOs: amortized O(1), the classic.", "description": "Problem: implement a queue (enqueue, dequeue, peek, empty) using only stacks and standard stack operations.\n\nSolution: inbox/outbox as described in the operations record. Enqueue pushes to inbox. Dequeue: if outbox is empty, pour inbox into outbox (reversing order), then pop outbox. Peek mirrors dequeue without popping. Each element is pushed once, moved once, popped once — 3n touches for n operations, amortized O(1) each; a single dequeue can cost O(n) when it triggers the pour. The key insight to state: the pour reverses twice (once into inbox order, once into outbox), restoring FIFO.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Implement a queue using two stacks with enqueue, dequeue, peek, and empty.", "solution": "Inbox stack for enqueue; outbox stack for dequeue. When outbox is empty, pour inbox into outbox (reversing to FIFO order), then pop. Each element moves at most twice: amortized O(1) per operation, O(n) worst single dequeue. Peek = same as dequeue without the pop.", "code": "class MyQueue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n    def _pour(self):\n        while self.inbox:\n            self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n    def dequeue(self):\n        if not self.outbox:\n            self._pour()\n        return self.outbox.pop()\n    def peek(self):\n        if not self.outbox:\n            self._pour()\n        return self.outbox[-1]\n    def empty(self):\n        return not self.inbox and not self.outbox\n\nq = MyQueue()\nfor v in [1, 2, 3]: q.enqueue(v)\nprint(q.dequeue(), q.peek(), q.empty())  # 1 2 False\n", "time": "O(1) amortized", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000018", "title": "Interview: Sliding Window Maximum", "d": "Max of every k-window in O(n) with a monotonic deque.", "description": "Problem: given array nums and window size k, return the max of each sliding window. Example: nums=[1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7], k=3 → [3,3,5,5,6,7].\n\nSolution: maintain a deque of indices with decreasing values (a monotonic queue). For each new index i: drop indices \u003c i−k+1 from the front (outside the window), then drop from the back while nums[back] \u003c= nums[i] (they can never be a window max while i is present), then append i. The front always holds the window's max. Each index enters and leaves the deque once: O(n) total. The brute force recomputes each window's max in O(k) for O(n·k); a heap gives O(n log k). The deque's trick is discarding dominated candidates eagerly — an element smaller than a newer element in the same window is useless forever.", "ladder": 3, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Return the maximum of every contiguous window of size k in O(n).", "solution": "Monotonic deque of indices with decreasing values. Per index i: evict front indices outside the window, evict back indices with value \u003c= nums[i] (dominated forever), append i; front is the window max. Each index enters/leaves once: O(n) time, O(k) space.", "code": "from collections import deque\ndef max_sliding_window(nums, k):\n    dq, out = deque(), []\n    for i, v in enumerate(nums):\n        while dq and dq[0] \u003c= i - k:\n            dq.popleft()\n        while dq and nums[dq[-1]] \u003c= v:\n            dq.pop()\n        dq.append(i)\n        if i >= k - 1:\n            out.append(nums[dq[0]])\n    return out\n\nprint(max_sliding_window([1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7], 3))\n# [3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 7]\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(k)"}, "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000019", "title": "Interview: Number of Recent Calls", "d": "Count pings in the last 3000ms with a timestamp queue: O(1) amortized.", "description": "Problem: implement ping(t) that records a call at time t (ms, strictly increasing) and returns the number of calls in [t−3000, t].\n\nSolution: keep a queue of timestamps. On ping(t), enqueue t, then dequeue from the front while the front is \u003c t−3000. Return the queue size. Because t increases, each timestamp is enqueued once and dequeued once: O(1) amortized per ping, O(w) space for w calls in the window. The queue's FIFO order is exactly what makes the front-eviction correct — the oldest timestamps are always at the head. A list with pop(0) would be O(n) per ping; the deque makes it O(1).", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "ping(t) records a call at ms t (increasing) and returns the count of calls in [t-3000, t].", "solution": "Queue of timestamps. Enqueue t; dequeue from front while front \u003c t-3000; return size. FIFO order guarantees the head holds the oldest calls, so front-eviction is exact. Each timestamp enters/leaves once: O(1) amortized per ping.", "code": "from collections import deque\nclass RecentCounter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.q = deque()\n    def ping(self, t):\n        self.q.append(t)\n        while self.q[0] \u003c t - 3000:\n            self.q.popleft()\n        return len(self.q)\n\nrc = RecentCounter()\nprint([rc.ping(t) for t in [1, 100, 3001, 3002]])  # [1, 2, 3, 3]\n", "time": "O(1) amortized", "space": "O(w)"}, "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000020", "title": "Interview: Reverse First K Elements of a Queue", "d": "Reverse the front k with a stack assist: O(n) time, O(k) space.", "description": "Problem: given a queue, reverse the order of its first k elements, leaving the rest in place. Example: [1,2,3,4,5], k=3 → [3,2,1,4,5].\n\nSolution: dequeue the first k elements onto a stack (O(k)), then dequeue the remaining n−k elements into a holding queue. Now enqueue the stack's contents (popping gives reversed order), then enqueue the held elements. Total: O(n) time, O(k) auxiliary for the stack plus O(n−k) for the hold — O(n) space, or O(k) if you rotate the tail in place instead of holding it. The stack is the natural reversal tool: its LIFO undoes the queue's FIFO for exactly the prefix you choose.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Reverse the first k elements of a queue, keeping the rest in order.", "solution": "Dequeue k elements onto a stack, hold the remaining n-k in a temp queue, then enqueue the stack (popped = reversed) followed by the held tail. O(n) time, O(n) space (O(k) with in-place tail rotation).", "code": "from collections import deque\ndef reverse_first_k(q, k):\n    st, n = [], len(q)\n    for _ in range(k):\n        st.append(q.popleft())\n    rest = [q.popleft() for _ in range(n - k)]\n    while st:\n        q.append(st.pop())\n    for x in rest:\n        q.append(x)\n    return q\n\nprint(list(reverse_first_k(deque([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]), 3)))\n# [3, 2, 1, 4, 5]\n", "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000021", "title": "Interview: Generate Binary Numbers with a Queue", "d": "BFS over the binary tree of strings: 1..n in order, O(n) total.", "description": "Problem: print binary representations of 1..n in order using a queue.\n\nSolution: enqueue '1'. Repeat n times: dequeue s, output s, enqueue s+'0' and s+'1'. This is breadth-first generation: level k of the implicit binary tree holds exactly the k-bit strings in order, and the queue's FIFO discipline visits levels left to right. Each of the n outputs costs O(1) queue work plus O(length) string building — O(n log n) total characters, which is optimal since the output itself is that big. The same pattern generates any BFS-ordered sequence: the queue turns recursion-shaped generation into level order.", "ladder": 2, "group": "interview", "language": "python", "interview": {"problem": "Generate binary strings for 1..n in increasing order using a queue.", "solution": "Enqueue '1'; n times: dequeue s, emit s, enqueue s+'0', s+'1'. FIFO order yields level-by-level (BFS) generation of the implicit binary tree. O(total output size) time, O(n) queue space.", "code": "from collections import deque\ndef binary_numbers(n):\n    q, out = deque(['1']), []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        s = q.popleft()\n        out.append(s)\n        q.append(s + '0')\n        q.append(s + '1')\n    return out\n\nprint(binary_numbers(5))  # ['1', '10', '11', '100', '101']\n", "time": "O(n log n) chars", "space": "O(n)"}, "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000022", "title": "Deque (Double-Ended Queue)", "d": "Both ends open: the queue and stack unified in one structure.", "description": "A deque opens both ends: append, appendleft, pop, popleft, all O(1). It's the generalization that contains both the queue (use one end for in, the other for out) and the stack (use one end for both). CPython implements it as a doubly-linked list of 64-element blocks — O(1) ends, good locality, no wraparound arithmetic, no resize pauses.\n\nDeques earn their keep in algorithms that need both ends: sliding-window maximum (pop left when out of window, pop right to maintain monotonicity), palindromes (compare and strip both ends), undo histories (push states, popleft to cap memory), and 0-1 BFS (pushleft for weight-0 edges). Java's ArrayDeque and C++'s std::deque are the native equivalents. If you find yourself wanting 'a queue, but sometimes from the other end,' stop fighting and use the deque.", "ladder": 2, "group": "variants", "variants": "Deque: O(1) both ends. Generalizes queue and stack. Backs sliding-window max, palindromes, undo buffers, 0-1 BFS.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000023", "title": "Priority Queue", "d": "FIFO by importance: the queue that serves the most urgent first.", "description": "A priority queue keeps the queue's interface (enqueue, dequeue) but changes the service order: dequeue returns the highest-priority element, not the oldest. It's usually built on a binary heap — enqueue O(log n), dequeue O(log n), peek O(1) — trading the queue's O(1) ends for order-by-importance. Python's heapq, Java's PriorityQueue, and C++'s priority_queue are the standard bearers.\n\nUse it for scheduling (highest-priority job first), Dijkstra's algorithm (closest unvisited node), event simulation (next event by time), and merging sorted streams. The honest trade: within equal priorities, order is unspecified unless you add a sequence number as a tiebreaker — a common production bug is assuming FIFO among equals. A priority queue is not 'a queue with sorting'; it's a different contract, and this database's heap section covers its internals.", "ladder": 2, "group": "variants", "variants": "Priority queue: dequeue by priority, not arrival. Heap-backed: O(log n) both ends. Needs sequence tiebreakers for FIFO-among-equals.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000024", "title": "Circular Queue (Ring Buffer)", "d": "The fixed-size workhorse: bounded, allocation-free, forever reusable.", "description": "The circular queue is the bounded queue's final form: a fixed array, head/tail indices modulo capacity, a size counter, zero allocation after construction. Network stacks, audio drivers, embedded firmware, and logging pipelines live on ring buffers because the memory footprint is known at creation and can never grow — when allocation is forbidden (interrupt handlers, real-time loops), the ring buffer is often the only legal queue.\n\nThe full/empty disambiguation (counter vs wasted slot) and the wraparound arithmetic are the entire implementation; everything else is policy. Lock-free single-producer/single-consumer rings (one writer owns tail, one reader owns head) are the backbone of high-performance messaging — LMAX Disruptor style — moving millions of events per second with no locks. Master the ring buffer and you own systems programming's favorite queue.", "ladder": 2, "group": "variants", "variants": "Circular queue: fixed array + modulo indices, O(1) ends, zero post-construction allocation. SPSC lock-free rings power high-throughput messaging.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000025", "title": "Queues in the Real World", "d": "Where FIFO runs the world: schedulers, printers, routers, and BFS.", "description": "Operating systems schedule ready processes in queues — round-robin literally cycles a queue, giving each process a time slice before re-enqueueing it. Printers spool jobs in queues so ten users share one printer fairly. Network routers buffer packets in queues; when the queue fills, tail-drop is the internet's default congestion signal. Call centers, ticket systems, and checkout lines are queues made of people.\n\nIn algorithms, the queue is breadth-first search: enqueue the start, dequeue a node, enqueue its unvisited neighbors, repeat — the FIFO order is exactly what explores level by level and finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs. Message brokers (RabbitMQ, Kafka partitions) are distributed queues; async task systems (Celery) are queues with workers; keyboard and event loops are queues of things that happened. Whenever the world produces events over time and something must handle them in order, there's a queue in the middle.", "ladder": 1, "group": "real-world", "real_world": "OS scheduling, print spooling, router buffers, BFS, message brokers, task queues, event loops, call centers, ticket systems.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000026", "title": "Queue Visualization: Circular Buffer", "d": "Watch the head and tail orbit: slots reused, nothing ever moves.", "description": "The visualization shows a circular buffer of capacity 5 holding three elements, with head at index 1 and tail at index 4. Drawn as a ring: slots 1, 2, 3 occupied, slots 4 and 0 empty, and the tail pointer about to wrap from index 4 back to index 0 on the next enqueue. The ring drawing matters — a straight line of slots suggests the queue marches off the end, but the modulo arithmetic bends it into a circle.\n\nFollow the two pointers around the ring. Enqueue writes at tail and advances tail = (tail+1) mod 5; dequeue reads at head and advances head = (head+1) mod 5. The count of live elements is (tail - head) mod 5 — when head == tail the buffer is empty, and the classic implementations either keep an explicit size counter or sacrifice one slot so that 'full' and 'empty' never look alike.\n\nTrace three more enqueues on the diagram: tail moves 4 -> 0 -> 1, and now tail == head with the size counter at 5 — full. One more enqueue would be an error (or a resize in a dynamic design). This ring is the entire circular buffer: two indices, one modulo, zero shifting — which is exactly why queues don't pay the array's O(n) dequeue tax.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "complexity_notes": "The diagram shows the whole trick: two orbiting pointers, zero element moves, O(1) everything.", "visualization": "slots:  [ 0 ] [ 1 ] [ 2 ] [ 3 ] [ 4 ]\n         (d)   (e)   (c)   (d)   (e)   \u003c- stored values\n                 ^               ^\n                head            tail\n          (next dequeue)   (next enqueue)\n\nlogical order: c -> d -> e -> f   (head=2, tail=1, n=4)\n\nenqueue(g): writes at slot 1, tail -> 2. Queue full (n=5).\n  [g][e][c][d][e]  head=2 tail=2  (full: n == capacity)\n\ndequeue(): reads slot 2 (c), head -> 3. Slot 2 is now free\nfor a future enqueue to reuse — no shifting, ever.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000027", "title": "Queue Pitfalls and Gotchas", "d": "Empty-dequeue, the ambiguous full, and list.pop(0) in a loop.", "description": "The number-one queue bug is dequeueing (or peeking) an empty queue — guard with isEmpty or use blocking variants that wait instead of crashing. Number two is the circular buffer's full/empty ambiguity: head == tail means both, so you need the size counter or the wasted slot, and you must test the exact transitions (fill-to-full then one more enqueue; drain-to-empty then one more dequeue).\n\nThe performance classic is Python's list.pop(0) in a loop — O(n) per pop, O(n²) total. It looks like a queue and acts like a queue and quietly burns your runtime; collections.deque.popleft is the O(1) fix. In concurrent code, 'if not q.empty(): q.get()' is a race — another thread can steal the item between the check and the get; use get() with timeout or the blocking form. And never let an unbounded queue sit between a fast producer and a slow consumer in production: that's a memory leak wearing a data structure costume.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000028", "title": "Queue Cheat Sheet: Operations and Complexities", "d": "Every queue operation, its complexity, and the one-line reason.", "description": "Enqueue: O(1) — write at tail, advance pointer. Dequeue: O(1) — read at head, advance pointer. Peek: O(1) — read head without moving. isEmpty/size: O(1) — maintained counter. All worst-case, not amortized, on a circular buffer — the ends-only restriction means no operation ever visits more than one element. Unbounded growth adds amortized O(1) via doubling, the dynamic-array argument.\n\nDeque extras: appendleft/pop from the left, all O(1). Priority queue: enqueue/dequeue O(log n) — different contract, heap-backed. Space: circular buffer costs exactly capacity × element_size; linked versions pay a pointer per element. The card is short because the structure is honest: two pointers, one rule, no surprises.", "ladder": 2, "group": "operations", "operations": [{"op": "enqueue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "write at tail, advance"}, {"op": "dequeue", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read at head, advance"}, {"op": "peek", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "read head, no advance"}, {"op": "isEmpty / size", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "maintained counter"}, {"op": "deque both ends", "complexity": "O(1)", "note": "appendleft / pop / popleft"}, {"op": "priority queue ops", "complexity": "O(log n)", "note": "heap-backed, different contract"}], "complexity_notes": "Worst-case O(1) core ops on circular buffers; the ends-only interface is what makes the guarantee unconditional.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000029", "title": "Queues vs Stacks", "d": "FIFO against LIFO: the same two pointers, opposite philosophies.", "description": "Queues and stacks are mirror images: both restrict access to the ends, but the queue serves the oldest (FIFO) while the stack serves the newest (LIFO). The queue models fairness — checkout lines, scheduling, BFS; the stack models nesting — function calls, undo, DFS, expression parsing. Implementation-wise they're twins: a deque does both jobs, and each can simulate the other (two stacks make a queue, two queues make a stack, both amortized O(1)).\n\nThe choice is about the order your problem needs. Level-by-level? Queue (BFS). Depth-first? Stack (DFS). Arrival order? Queue. Most-recent-first? Stack. When in doubt, ask what 'next' means in your domain — the answer picks the structure. And remember the simulation results: the difference is discipline, not power; either can do the other's job with constant-factor overhead.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000030", "title": "Queues vs Lists", "d": "Restricted ends against random access: why the queue's limits are its strength.", "description": "A general list (Python list, ArrayList) can do everything a queue does — append at the end, pop(0) at the front — but pop(0) is O(n) because it shifts every element. The queue's circular buffer does the same job in O(1) by never moving elements, just orbiting pointers. The list buys random access and pays with shifting; the queue surrenders random access and earns O(1) ends.\n\nIn practice: Python's list is a fine stack (append/pop at the end, both O(1) amortized) but a terrible queue (pop(0) is O(n)); collections.deque is the real queue. The deeper lesson: a narrower interface enables a faster implementation. The queue promises less than the list and delivers its promises cheaper — reach for it whenever ends-only access is all you need.", "ladder": 2, "group": "overview", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000031", "title": "Queue Drill 1: Hot Potato Simulation", "d": "Josephus-lite: rotate the queue, eliminate every kth player.", "description": "Task: n players in a circle pass a potato; every kth holder is eliminated. Simulate with a queue for n=7, k=3. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: enqueue players 1..7. Repeat: rotate k−1 times (dequeue then enqueue — moves the front to the back), then dequeue once to eliminate. The queue's rotation models the circle; elimination is just a dequeue. Each elimination costs O(k) rotations, n eliminations → O(n·k) total.\n\nSolution: elimination order 3, 6, 2, 7, 5, 1; survivor 4. The rotate-by-dequeue-enqueue idiom is worth memorizing — it's how you cycle any queue, and it appears in round-robin schedulers and card tricks alike.", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "from collections import deque\ndef hot_potato(n, k):\n    q = deque(range(1, n + 1))\n    out = []\n    while len(q) > 1:\n        for _ in range(k - 1):\n            q.append(q.popleft())  # rotate\n        out.append(q.popleft())    # eliminate\n    return out, q[0]\n\nprint(hot_potato(7, 3))  # ([3, 6, 2, 7, 5, 1], 4)", "language": "python", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000032", "title": "Queue Drill 2: Palindrome Check with Queue + Stack", "d": "Feed both structures and compare: FIFO vs LIFO as a mirror test.", "description": "Task: check whether 'racecar' is a palindrome using one queue and one stack. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: enqueue every character into the queue and push every character onto the stack. Then repeatedly dequeue from the queue (front-to-back order) and pop from the stack (back-to-front order) and compare. All pairs equal ⟺ palindrome. The queue walks forward, the stack walks backward — the two disciplines are literally mirror images here.\n\nSolution: 'racecar' → True; 'hello' → False. O(n) time, O(n) space. (The two-pointer version is O(1) space, but this drill's point is feeling FIFO and LIFO as opposite traversals of the same data.)", "ladder": 1, "group": "practice", "program": "from collections import deque\ndef is_palindrome(s):\n    q, st = deque(s), list(s)\n    return all(q.popleft() == st.pop() for _ in s)\n\nprint(is_palindrome('racecar'))  # True\nprint(is_palindrome('hello'))    # False", "language": "python", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000033", "title": "Queue Drill 3: Print Level by Level", "d": "Use a queue's size snapshot to separate BFS levels.", "description": "Task: given a binary tree, print values level by level using a queue. Work the snapshot idea by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: enqueue the root. While the queue is non-empty: record its current size (that's exactly the number of nodes on this level — nothing enqueued during the level changes it), dequeue that many nodes, printing each and enqueueing their children. The size snapshot is the trick: it freezes the level boundary before the children of this level start arriving.\n\nSolution pattern below. O(n) time, O(w) space for max width w. This is the canonical BFS-levels template — memorize it; half of all BFS interview problems are variations.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "from collections import deque\ndef levels(root):\n    if not root: return []\n    q, out = deque([root]), []\n    while q:\n        lvl = []\n        for _ in range(len(q)):      # snapshot: this level only\n            node = q.popleft()\n            lvl.append(node[0])\n            q.extend(node[1])        # children\n        out.append(lvl)\n    return out\n\n# tree as (value, [children])\nt = (1, [(2, [(4, []), (5, [])]), (3, [(6, [])])])\nprint(levels(t))  # [[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]", "language": "python", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000034", "title": "Queue Drill 4: Interleave Halves with a Stack Twist", "d": "Reverse the second half with a stack, then weave: [1,2,3,4] into [1,4,2,3].", "description": "Task: given queue [1,2,3,4] (even length), reverse the second half using a stack, then interleave the two halves: expected [1,4,2,3]. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: dequeue the first half (1,2) into a holding list — popleft preserves order, so hold = [1,2]. Dequeue the second half (3,4) onto a stack, then pop the stack back off: 4, then 3 — the stack reversed it to [4,3]. Now weave alternately: take from hold, take from the reversed half → 1, 4, 2, 3.\n\nSolution: [1,4,2,3]. The drill's real lesson is choreographing two structures — the stack reverses, the queue preserves order — a skill every 'rearrange with limited tools' problem needs.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "from collections import deque\ndef interleave_twist(q):\n    n = len(q)\n    first = [q.popleft() for _ in range(n // 2)]  # [1, 2], order kept\n    st = [q.popleft() for _ in range(n // 2)]     # [3, 4] onto stack\n    second = [st.pop() for _ in range(len(st))]    # [4, 3] reversed\n    res = deque()\n    for a, b in zip(first, second):\n        res.append(a)\n        res.append(b)\n    return res\n\nprint(list(interleave_twist(deque([1, 2, 3, 4]))))  # [1, 4, 2, 3]", "language": "python", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000035", "title": "Queue Drill 5: Sliding Window Average", "d": "Running average over the last k values: queue + running sum, O(1) per step.", "description": "Task: stream values 4, 8, 15, 16, 23 with window k=3; after each value, report the average of the last 3. Work it by hand, then check.\n\nWalkthrough: keep a queue of the window and a running sum. On each new value: enqueue it, add to sum; if the queue exceeds k, dequeue the oldest and subtract it. The average is sum/k (or sum/len for the partial first windows). Each step is O(1) — no rescanning the window.\n\nSolution: after 4 → 4.0; 8 → 6.0; 15 → 9.0; 16 → 13.0; 23 → 18.0. This is the streaming pattern behind moving averages in finance, sensor smoothing, and rate limiters — the queue holds the window, the sum avoids the rescan.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "from collections import deque\ndef moving_avg(stream, k):\n    q, s, out = deque(), 0, []\n    for v in stream:\n        q.append(v); s += v\n        if len(q) > k:\n            s -= q.popleft()\n        out.append(s / len(q))\n    return out\n\nprint(moving_avg([4, 8, 15, 16, 23], 3))\n# [4.0, 6.0, 9.0, 13.0, 18.0]", "language": "python", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000036", "title": "Queue History, Naming and Standard Library Homes", "d": "FIFO is older than computers: from print spoolers to Kafka.", "description": "The queue predates electronic computing — punched-card job batches in the 1950s were physical queues, and the term 'spool' (Simultaneous Peripheral Operations On-Line) described queueing print jobs while the CPU worked. Dijkstra's THE operating system (1968) used semaphores to guard queues; Unix pipes are byte queues between processes; message brokers turned the humble queue into planetary infrastructure.\n\nWhere to find them: Python collections.deque and queue.Queue, JavaScript arrays (or a head-index wrapper), Java ArrayDeque / LinkedBlockingQueue, C++ std::queue (deque adapter), C# Queue\u003cT>, Go channels (typed queues with synchronization built in), Rust VecDeque. Note the split every ecosystem makes: a fast sequential queue and a thread-safe blocking queue are different types with different contracts — pick by concurrency, not habit.", "ladder": 1, "group": "overview", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000037", "title": "Queue Memory Math: Space Accounting", "d": "Exactly what a queue costs: capacity slots, two indices, one counter.", "description": "A circular-buffer queue of capacity c holding element_size-byte elements costs exactly c × element_size bytes for the slots plus ~24 bytes for head, tail, count, and the base pointer. A queue holding 1,000 8-byte values in a 1,024-slot buffer costs 8 KB — fixed at construction, never growing, never allocating. That determinism is why rings own embedded and real-time work.\n\nThe linked version costs n × (element_size + pointer + allocator header) — for a million small elements, easily 3–5x the circular buffer. The unbounded growing queue sits between: amortized doubling keeps waste under 2x, but the worst-case footprint is transiently 3x during a resize (old buffer + new buffer + in-flight copies). Size your bounded queues from the actual producer/consumer rates: capacity ≈ (peak_rate − drain_rate) × burst_duration, plus headroom.", "ladder": 3, "group": "circular-buffer", "complexity_notes": "Circular: c * element_size + O(1). Linked: n * (element + pointer + alloc). Unbounded transient peak ~3x during doubling.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000038", "title": "Queue Testing Checklist", "d": "The queue gauntlet: empty, full, wraparound, and interleavings.", "description": "Empty transitions: dequeue/peek on empty must raise or block — test it. Fill-to-full: enqueue exactly capacity items, then one more — the policy (raise/block/drop) must trigger exactly there, not one early or late. Wraparound: enqueue 3, dequeue 2, enqueue 3 more in a capacity-4 buffer — then drain and verify FIFO order survived the wrap. Interleavings: alternate enqueues and dequeues randomly against a model (a Python list as oracle) for thousands of steps.\n\nSize accounting: after every operation, len(queue) must equal enqueues − dequeues; assert it in tests. Single-element queue (capacity 1): the hardest case for full/empty disambiguation — hammer it. For blocking queues: producer-faster-than-consumer (blocks correctly), consumer-faster (waits correctly), and shutdown semantics (no lost items, no hangs). Randomized model-based testing catches the wraparound bugs that example tests miss.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "program": "from collections import deque\nimport random\ndef fuzz(steps=5000):\n    from collections import deque\n    class Q:\n        def __init__(s, c):\n            s.a=[None]*c; s.h=s.t=s.n=0\n        def enq(s, x):\n            assert s.n \u003c len(s.a), 'overflow undetected'\n            s.a[s.t]=x; s.t=(s.t+1)%len(s.a); s.n+=1\n        def deq(s):\n            assert s.n > 0, 'underflow undetected'\n            x=s.a[s.h]; s.h=(s.h+1)%len(s.a); s.n-=1; return x\n    q, model = Q(7), deque()\n    rng = random.Random(42)\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        if model and rng.random() \u003c 0.5:\n            assert q.deq() == model.popleft()\n        elif len(model) \u003c 7:\n            v = rng.randrange(1000); q.enq(v); model.append(v)\n        assert q.n == len(model)\n    print('fuzz ok:', steps, 'steps')\nfuzz()", "language": "python", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000039", "title": "Queue Debugging Guide", "d": "Diagnosing queue corruption: order violations, phantom fulls, and lost wakeups.", "description": "Order violations (dequeue returns the wrong item): the head pointer advanced wrong — print (head, tail, n) after every operation in a small test and watch for the invariant 'n == (tail − head) mod capacity'. If n drifts from the true count, the counter updates are asymmetric — one path enqueues without incrementing.\n\nPhantom full (enqueue raises but the queue looks empty): the full/empty disambiguation is broken — head == tail with no counter means you can't tell; add the counter. Phantom empty is the mirror. Wraparound bugs show up only after enough operations to lap the buffer — if failures start after exactly 'capacity' operations, suspect the modulo.\n\nConcurrency symptoms: lost items (check-then-act race — use the queue's atomic get), hangs (consumer waiting on a queue the producer thinks is full — mismatched capacity or forgotten notify), and the unbounded queue that eats all memory (producer/consumer rate mismatch — add metrics on queue depth and alert on growth). Depth metrics are the cheapest queue debugger in production: graph them.", "ladder": 2, "group": "practice", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}, {"id": "JAH-QUE-000040", "title": "Queue Performance Benchmarks", "d": "Measured: deque vs list-pop(0) vs circular buffer, and where the time goes.", "description": "Benchmark on a typical machine, one million operations: collections.deque append+popleft ≈ 0.15 s; list append + pop(0) ≈ 20+ s (the O(n) shift dominates — 100x slower and quadratic, so it gets worse); a hand-rolled circular buffer in Python ≈ 0.8 s (modulo and method overhead); the same ring in C ≈ 0.02 s. The lesson isn't 'C is fast' — it's that pop(0) changes the complexity class while the others don't.\n\nThroughput ceilings: a lock-free SPSC ring in C moves 10–50M messages/sec; a mutex-guarded queue ~5–10M; Python's queue.Queue ~0.5M puts/sec (GIL + locking). Batching (dequeue up to k per lock acquisition) is the standard throughput trick. And the queue-depth metric again: p99 latency in a pipeline is a function of queue depth × service time — Little's Law (L = λW) turns 'how deep is the queue' into 'how late is the output'.", "ladder": 3, "group": "complexity", "program": "import timeit\nfrom collections import deque\nn = 200000\nt_deque = timeit.timeit('q.append(1); q.popleft()',\n                      setup='from collections import deque; q=deque([0])', number=n)\nt_list = timeit.timeit('q.append(1); q.pop(0)',\n                     setup='q=[0]', number=20000)\nprint(f'deque x{n}: {t_deque:.3f}s')\nprint(f'list.pop(0) x20000: {t_list:.3f}s  \u003c- O(n) each, quadratic total')", "language": "python", "complexity_notes": "deque O(1) ends ~0.15s/M ops; list.pop(0) O(n) each — complexity class dominates every constant.", "src": "reference-authored", "last_verified": "2026-10-19"}];
var OPS=[["enqueue", "O(1)", "write at tail, advance with wraparound; no element moves", "Enqueue adds an element at the tail. In the circular-buffer implementation, that means writing to slots[tail] and advancing tail = (tail + 1) % capacity — two operations, no element moves, no searching. It is O(1) time and O(1) extra space, unconditionally: unlike array append there is no amortization subtlety, because the buffer is pre-allocated and the write position is always known."], ["enqueue (growing)", "O(1) amortized", "unbounded queues double the buffer when full", "Enqueue adds an element at the tail. In the circular-buffer implementation, that means writing to slots[tail] and advancing tail = (tail + 1) % capacity — two operations, no element moves, no searching. It is O(1) time and O(1) extra space, unconditionally: unlike array append there is no amortization subtlety, because the buffer is pre-allocated and the write position is always known."], ["dequeue", "O(1)", "read at head, advance with wraparound; the freed slot is reused", "Dequeue removes and returns the head element — the one that has waited longest. In the circular buffer, that means reading slots[head] and advancing head = (head + 1) % capacity. Like enqueue it touches exactly one element: O(1) time, O(1) space. The FIFO guarantee is just the observation that head only moves forward through arrival order, so the longest-waiting element is always served next."], ["peek / front", "O(1)", "read slots[head] without moving the pointer", "Peek (front) returns the head element without advancing the pointer — a read-only look at who's next. It costs O(1) and changes nothing, which makes it the safe way to inspect before deciding: a scheduler can peek at the next job's priority, a parser can peek at the next token, a BFS can check the frontier's depth. Like dequeue, peeking an empty queue is an error."], ["isEmpty", "O(1)", "size counter == 0; or head == tail with empty/full disambiguation", "isEmpty and size look trivial, but their implementation decides a real bug class. In a circular buffer, head == tail is ambiguous — it means both empty and full. The standard fixes are a size counter (increment on enqueue, decrement on dequeue) or a permanently wasted slot (declare full when the next tail would hit head). The counter is clearer and makes size O(1); recomputing size by walking would be O(n) and is never acceptable."], ["size", "O(1)", "maintained counter — never recomputed by walking", "isEmpty and size look trivial, but their implementation decides a real bug class. In a circular buffer, head == tail is ambiguous — it means both empty and full. The standard fixes are a size counter (increment on enqueue, decrement on dequeue) or a permanently wasted slot (declare full when the next tail would hit head). The counter is clearer and makes size O(1); recomputing size by walking would be O(n) and is never acceptable."], ["enqueue (bounded, full)", "O(1) decision", "block / drop-newest / drop-oldest / grow — pick deliberately", "A bounded queue picks a capacity up front. When it's full, someone must decide: block the producer until space frees (backpressure — the pipeline slows down honestly instead of exploding), drop the newest arrival (tail drop — fine when stale data is worthless), or drop the oldest (head drop — keeps the freshest data, right for sensor streams and live dashboards). Python's queue.Queue(maxsize=N) blocks; deque(maxlen=N) drops the oldest silently."], ["enqueue (unbounded, full)", "O(1) amortized", "double the buffer like a dynamic array", "A bounded queue picks a capacity up front. When it's full, someone must decide: block the producer until space frees (backpressure — the pipeline slows down honestly instead of exploding), drop the newest arrival (tail drop — fine when stale data is worthless), or drop the oldest (head drop — keeps the freshest data, right for sensor streams and live dashboards). Python's queue.Queue(maxsize=N) blocks; deque(maxlen=N) drops the oldest silently."], ["append / appendleft", "O(1)", "enqueue at either end", "A deque (double-ended queue) allows enqueue and dequeue at both ends: append/appendleft, pop/popleft, all O(1). It's implemented as a doubly-linked list or — as in CPython — a doubly-linked list of fixed-size blocks, which gives O(1) ends with good cache behavior and no wraparound bookkeeping. Every queue is a deque with one end tied behind its back; every stack is a deque using one end."], ["pop / popleft", "O(1)", "dequeue from either end", "A deque (double-ended queue) allows enqueue and dequeue at both ends: append/appendleft, pop/popleft, all O(1). It's implemented as a doubly-linked list or — as in CPython — a doubly-linked list of fixed-size blocks, which gives O(1) ends with good cache behavior and no wraparound bookkeeping. Every queue is a deque with one end tied behind its back; every stack is a deque using one end."], ["peek either end", "O(1)", "q[0] / q[-1]", "A deque (double-ended queue) allows enqueue and dequeue at both ends: append/appendleft, pop/popleft, all O(1). It's implemented as a doubly-linked list or — as in CPython — a doubly-linked list of fixed-size blocks, which gives O(1) ends with good cache behavior and no wraparound bookkeeping. Every queue is a deque with one end tied behind its back; every stack is a deque using one end."], ["enqueue / dequeue / peek / size", "O(1) worst-case", "single slot + single index advance; no dependence on n", "The O(1) bounds for queues are unusually clean because they rest on a pointer argument, not amortization. Enqueue writes exactly one slot and advances one index. Dequeue reads exactly one slot and advances one index. Peek reads one slot. Size reads one counter. No operation's work grows with n, so every bound is worst-case O(1), not amortized — the only exception is the unbounded queue's doubling resize, which is amortized O(1) by the same accounting argument as dynamic arrays."], ["enqueue (unbounded resize)", "O(1) amortized", "doubling; same accounting as dynamic arrays", "The O(1) bounds for queues are unusually clean because they rest on a pointer argument, not amortization. Enqueue writes exactly one slot and advances one index. Dequeue reads exactly one slot and advances one index. Peek reads one slot. Size reads one counter. No operation's work grows with n, so every bound is worst-case O(1), not amortized — the only exception is the unbounded queue's doubling resize, which is amortized O(1) by the same accounting argument as dynamic arrays."], ["peek", "O(1)", "read head, no advance", "Enqueue: O(1) — write at tail, advance pointer. Dequeue: O(1) — read at head, advance pointer. Peek: O(1) — read head without moving. isEmpty/size: O(1) — maintained counter. All worst-case, not amortized, on a circular buffer — the ends-only restriction means no operation ever visits more than one element. Unbounded growth adds amortized O(1) via doubling, the dynamic-array argument."], ["isEmpty / size", "O(1)", "maintained counter", "Enqueue: O(1) — write at tail, advance pointer. Dequeue: O(1) — read at head, advance pointer. Peek: O(1) — read head without moving. isEmpty/size: O(1) — maintained counter. All worst-case, not amortized, on a circular buffer — the ends-only restriction means no operation ever visits more than one element. Unbounded growth adds amortized O(1) via doubling, the dynamic-array argument."], ["deque both ends", "O(1)", "appendleft / pop / popleft", "Enqueue: O(1) — write at tail, advance pointer. Dequeue: O(1) — read at head, advance pointer. Peek: O(1) — read head without moving. isEmpty/size: O(1) — maintained counter. All worst-case, not amortized, on a circular buffer — the ends-only restriction means no operation ever visits more than one element. Unbounded growth adds amortized O(1) via doubling, the dynamic-array argument."], ["priority queue ops", "O(log n)", "heap-backed, different contract", "Enqueue: O(1) — write at tail, advance pointer. Dequeue: O(1) — read at head, advance pointer. Peek: O(1) — read head without moving. isEmpty/size: O(1) — maintained counter. All worst-case, not amortized, on a circular buffer — the ends-only restriction means no operation ever visits more than one element. Unbounded growth adds amortized O(1) via doubling, the dynamic-array argument."]]; /* [op, complexity, note, why] */
